      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "To investigate and differentiate the causes of hyponatremia in an 8-y old boy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "An 8-y boy presented with headache, vomiting, and diplopia. Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain confirmed a mass in the pineal region. Pathology report demonstrated a mixed germ cell tumor with a yolk sac component. A multi-agent chemotherapy and radiation regimen was initiated. He developed hyponatremia, with sodium concentrations varying from 116 to 133 mEq/l."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Serum levels of sodium, chloride, phosphorous, uric acid, and osmolality were low. Serum α-fetoprotein, β-HCG, and lactate dehydrogenase were highly elevated. Urine sodium and osmolality were increased."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These presentations suggest that the patient has cerebral salt-wasting syndrome caused by intracranial germ cell tumor. Recognition and differentiation of cerebral salt-wasting syndrome from other disorders are essential."
          }
        ]
      },
